@scene(100, 5)

// A small procedural planet, 50 mm across, as one shape. The land is real
// geometry: a height field (mm) subtracted from the sphere's distance, built
// from the Lipschitz-bounded Bump noises so the result is still a distance
// bound the raymarcher, the mesher and the measurement tools can all use.
// Four octaves of fBm set the continents; ridged turbulence raises mountain
// chains, but only on land. Below sea level the surface is clamped flat and
// coloured by the depth of the (virtual) sea floor, so the shallows read
// light and the deeps dark without a second, transparent shape. Colours and
// materials band with height through measured sand, grass and snow over a
// granite rock, and snow also creeps down toward the poles. The texture and
// the height field are the library's natural.terrain.

radius = 25;

// Snow creeps down toward the poles: a bias on the snow line that grows over
// the top and bottom quarter of the sphere.
latitude = abs(p.z) / radius;
polar = 3 * clamp((latitude - 0.72) / 0.28, 0, 1);

// The terrain texture: continents 12 mm across, and the land's height in mm.
planetMaterial, planetColor, height = natural.terrain(12, polar);

planet = sphere(radius) - max(height, 0);
planet.material = planetMaterial;
planet.color = planetColor;

return planet;
